virtiorng.device beta
=====================

virtiorng.device is an entropy (random number) device for QEMU's
virtio-rng card (virtio-rng-pci), for AmigaOS 4.1 Final Edition running
in QEMU. A program opens it and reads random bytes with CMD_READ; the
bytes come from the host's random source.

virtio exists only in QEMU: this driver is not for real hardware.
Tested with QEMU 11.0.50 on the AmigaOne (-M amigaone), Pegasos II
(-M pegasos2) and Sam460ex (-M sam460ex) machines.

This archive holds two builds of the same driver. Use one of them.

  Kickstart/virtiorng.device   1.1, a Kickstart module, started at boot
  DEVS/virtiorng.device        1.2, loaded from DEVS: when first opened,
                               unloaded when no longer used
  rngread                      a Shell tool that checks the device


1. Give QEMU a virtio-rng card
------------------------------
Add to your QEMU command line:

    -device virtio-rng-pci

Up to four cards work; each is a unit (0 to 3), in the order found.


2a. Install the disk build (simplest)
-------------------------------------
    Copy DEVS/virtiorng.device DEVS:

Programs open "virtiorng.device", unit 0.


2b. Or install the Kickstart build
----------------------------------
Copy Kickstart/virtiorng.device into the Kickstart folder your boot
loader uses, and add this line to its Kicklayout file, inside the
configuration you boot (before the blank line that ends it):

    MODULE Kickstart/virtiorng.device

When booting QEMU with bboot, that folder and Kicklayout are inside the
kickstart zip you pass to QEMU: unpack it, add the module and the line,
and zip it again. At boot the driver checks itself and prints
"virtiorng.device: self-test PASSED" on the serial output.

Do not install both builds.


3. Check it
-----------
In a Shell, in this drawer:

    rngread

It runs nine checks (reads, aborts, flushes, recovery after a device
reset, an odd-sized read into an odd address) and prints one line for
each; the return code is 0 when all pass. "rngread 4096 1" reads 4 MB
from unit 1.


4. If something goes wrong
--------------------------
The driver reports what it does on the debug output, in lines that
start with "virtiorng.device". After a problem, in a Shell:

    DumpDebugBuffer >RAM:debug.txt

and report it with your QEMU version and command line.


Source: https://github.com/derfsss/VirtioRust (MIT licence)

